Artemis the Ranger wrote:
> 
> Some strategies I've discovered follow: (you may already have realized
> these things for yourself, or not):
> 
> - Always play an elf. You'll be chaotic (which I enjoy) but you'll
> have infrared vision. Infrared is invaluable for a Ranger (especially
> in the Gnomish Mines).

There are two sides to this story, an elf has a max strength
of 18 but WIS and INT of 20. If you don't plan to do a lot
of spell casting, an elf is a poor choice. With skill
possible only for healing, divination and escape spells,
you're not going to replace your bow with offensive spells.
You could get the infravision with a gnome or an orc if
you're having a lot of trouble with the early game.

Chaotic has some advantages in actions you can take that
your neutral counterpart might be penalized for. The
advantage to neutral is the selection of artifacts. If you
get an early wish, the only quest artifact a chaotic ranger
might benefit from is the Master Key of Thievery. A neutral
ranger would have a choice of 6 quest artifacts he might
wish for. 

As for gifts, the best chaotic artifact gift is StormBringer
where a neutral might hope for MagicBane, Mjollnir or
Vorpral Blade, all of which rank ahead of SB in my book.

> - First move of the game: #name your elven dagger Sting.

A good idea if you have trouble with the early game where
orcs are a threat. Once you set your sights higher (or would
that be lower?), it will make getting additional artifacts
more difficult. I never name Sting or Orchrist.

> <snip good advice>

> - Do the Gnomish Mines before the main dungeon branch.

If I have infravision or if the first level is lit, I will
do it first to get some better armor. A good light source or
"oESP might also be enough to make me try for minetown. I'll
probably do a little wet work near the stairs in any event,
trying to score some armor and a pick axe. Once I'm in a
situation where I can't see far, I'll turn back and head for
Sokoban.

I find a spear to be a useful weapon until I can find
something better. I advance dagger and bow skills to skilled
immediately but do some more serious thinking before
advancing both to expert. (those two would eat up 10 skill
slots, over half of my total. 

